Every improvement in human welfare — increased lifespan, reduced childhood mortality, freedom from famine, protection from disease — has come from the growth of knowledge, not from the direct pursuit of pleasure.
The scientific method, which is the systematic pursuit of knowledge over comfortable assumptions, has produced more value for human wellbeing than any other human institution.
Aristotle distinguished episteme (scientific knowledge) from merely holding true beliefs by luck — genuine knowledge enables prediction and control, capacities that mere pleasure cannot generate.
